/// Require historical lookback before computing a function result.
///
/// `@warmup(period)` associates a lookback requirement with an annotated
/// function so tooling can reason about the amount of prior data needed before
/// the function should be considered ready.
///
/// @param period Number of prior data points required before the annotated
/// function should be evaluated as warmed up. The argument is documentary: the
/// indicator signatures pass their own `period` parameter (e.g.
/// `@warmup(period)`, `@warmup(period + 1)`) to record the lookback alongside
/// the function.
/// @note `@warmup` is a pure marker annotation — it carries no lifecycle
/// handlers (`before`/`after`/`on_define`/`metadata`). Handlers were removed
/// for two reasons: (1) the former runtime before/after hooks were no-ops (the
/// pre-hook never populated the warmup state the post-hook checked) and their
/// wiring required every annotated function's series parameter to carry a
/// statically-proven typed-array element carrier, which the untyped stdlib
/// indicator signatures do not provide; (2) any lifecycle handler causes the
/// annotation argument to be compiled at module/definition scope, where the
/// function-parameter reference `period` is not bound — producing
/// "Undefined variable: period". A pure marker registers `@warmup` (so it
/// resolves at use sites) without evaluating the argument.
/// @see std::finance::annotations::indicator::@indicator
/// @example
/// @warmup(20)
/// pub fn sma(data: Column<number>, period: number) -> number {
///     return data.rolling(period).mean();
/// }
// ADR-009 C3-S6: config params declare their type on the collapsed
// annotation surface. `number` matches the indicator signatures' own
// `period: number` params (the documentary `@warmup(period)` spelling).
pub annotation warmup(period: number) {
}
